/* The Module Manager loads a module from an image whose preamble describes it: the size
   of its instruction area, the offsets or addresses of its entry functions, and the sizes
   of its data area and default thread stacks. Every one of those fields comes from the
   image. This test drives each loader with images whose preamble lies about them, and
   checks that the loader refuses before acting on the lie.

   Four things are checked.

   The length-checked loaders are given the image's length, and must refuse an image
   too short for its own preamble, or one whose declared instruction area runs past its
   end, before reading anything else. The image is placed so that its last byte is the
   last byte of a readable page and the next page is inaccessible: a loader that reads
   one byte too far takes a fault rather than returning, so the test cannot pass by
   reading past the image and then refusing.

   Every loader, old and new, must refuse entry functions that lie outside the module's
   instruction area, a missing callback function, an instruction area smaller than the
   preamble, and a preamble size larger than the instruction area.

   A preamble whose data or stack sizes overflow when the loader adds them up must be
   refused with the Module Manager's protection mutex released. The mutex here counts
   its owner, so a loader that returns holding it is caught at the next case.

   The file loader must not clear a module control block it is about to refuse as
   already loaded, and the in-place loader's alignment check must not release a mutex
   it does not hold.  */

#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <sys/mman.h>
#include <unistd.h>

#include "threadx_module_manager_host_test_port.h"

#include "tx_initialize.h"
